  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "Both pieces are in place: the dashboard exists, and the alert rule is enabled and its first run finished with status `ok`.\n\n**1. What's in the data.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request, with:\n- **Request:** `request`, `url`, `extension`, `referer`\n- **Response:** `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as a keyword string such as `\"503\"`) and `bytes`\n- **Client:** `clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os`, `machine.ram`\n- **Location:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`\n- **Other:** `host`, `tags`, `memory`, `hour_of_day`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-c45fcd06 Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-c45fcd06-web-logs-overview-fb7ace15f6a3`) has 7 panels:\n- Total requests (number)\n- Unique visitors (distinct `clientip` count)\n- Average bytes per request\n- Requests over time (line chart)\n- Requests by response code (pie chart)\n- Top requested URLs, with request count and total bytes (table)\n- Top 10 source countries by request count (bar chart)\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-c45fcd06 5xx spike\"** (id `14287ede-aed4-4c15-a53e-e7717c620f9f`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 requests with a 5xx code arrive in the last 5 minutes, based on `timestamp`. It counts a request as 5xx when its `response.keyword` value starts with \"5\".\n\n- **Query not tested against the data:** I tried to confirm that the query matches real 5xx records, but calling Kibana directly with the API key returned 401 Unauthorized. So it hasn't been checked against the data. A clean first run shows the rule executes; it doesn't prove the query finds anything.\n- **No notifications:** the rule has no actions attached, so when it fires it won't notify anyone. Add a connector (Slack, email, etc.) if you want alerts sent somewhere.",
